                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_gap_right:     `<strong>Right diagnosis.</strong> Summit's own survey points at unclear decision rights and weak cross-functional coordination, not a generic skills deficit. You've separated the presenting request from the demonstrated capability gap — now you can design for what will actually move the KPI.`,
                    coach_r1_gap_leadership: `<strong>You've taken the request at face value.</strong> Summit asked for “leadership training,” but its own data says the problem is structural — decision rights and coordination. Design 220 seats of leadership skills and you'll miss the KPI you promised to measure. What in the data told you this was a skills problem?`,
                    coach_r1_gap_technical: `<strong>This isn't a technology-knowledge gap.</strong> The managers don't need a grid-tech course — the survey describes coordination and decision-rights problems. Diagnose what the evidence shows, not what's easiest to teach.`,
                    coach_r1_gap_motivation: `<strong>“Just inspire them” is a comfortable answer.</strong> A pep-rally won't fix unclear decision rights and broken cross-functional coordination. The gap is capability and structure, not enthusiasm.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the coordination/decision-rights gap, kept the org-design part honest with the client, and committed to a behaviour-plus-KPI measure you can actually audit at 6–12 months. That is the diagnostic spine of a defensible bid.`,
                    coach_r1_alleducation:  `<strong>Education can't fix everything Summit raised.</strong> Some of this is org design — decision rights and structure — and a programme that promises to cure it will fail its own Level 3 audit. Move the skill and decision-confidence part; flag the rest honestly.`,
                    coach_r1_allorgdesign:  `<strong>Declining the education role entirely throws away a winnable engagement.</strong> Education genuinely moves the skill and decision-confidence part of this gap. The disciplined answer is mixed: solve what you can, flag what you can't.`,
                    coach_r1_measure_right: `<strong>That's a measure you can defend.</strong> A named operational KPI plus on-the-job behaviour change is exactly the Level 3–4 commitment Wasatch's impact-led brand is built on — provided your architecture forces the transfer to produce it.`,
                    coach_r1_measure_reaction: `<strong>Satisfaction and a knowledge test are Level 1–2.</strong> They're easy to hit and prove nothing about on-the-job change. Summit's CHRO is buying measured business impact, not a happy-sheet.`,
                    coach_r1_measure_vague: `<strong>“Improved leadership capability” is not a measure.</strong> With no defined metric you've signed up for an audit you can't pass. Name the behaviour and the operational KPI now.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>Designing on thin evidence is how you mis-scope.</strong> Without interrogating the survey data you'll default to the client's wording. Spend the diagnostic effort before you commit the design.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>Blended-with-digital is the right balance.</strong> A live-virtual core scales to 220 across the operations footprint, targeted in-person intensives carry the high-transfer work, and digital pre-work protects faculty time — all inside the ceiling.`,
                    coach_r2_inperson:      `<strong>Five in-person days for all 220 blows the ceiling.</strong> Faculty-day and travel costs alone run about $2.1M — you're roughly $300K underwater before materials. Quality is real, but so is the $1.8M cap. Which modules truly need the room?`,
                    coach_r2_virtual:       `<strong>All-virtual is cheap but transfers shallowly.</strong> It scales easily, but a screen-only journey won't move on-the-job behaviour — and you just promised a Level 3 KPI. Format is an outcomes decision, not only a cost one.`,
                    coach_r2_projectcoach:  `<strong>Application projects and coaching are what reach Level 3.</strong> Live work on the real grid transition plus manager coaching and spaced reinforcement is the mechanism that turns a workshop into measurable behaviour change.`,
                    coach_r2_transferfit:   `<strong>Design and transfer are aligned.</strong> A blended journey with live application projects, manager coaching and spaced reinforcement is exactly the architecture that makes the Level 3 promise credible — the format and the transfer mechanism reinforce each other.`,
                    coach_r2_assignment:    `<strong>A graded reflection is thin transfer.</strong> It feels like rigour, but a post-course essay rarely changes on-the-job behaviour. Without real application and reinforcement, your Level 3 promise has no engine.`,
                    coach_r2_notransfer:    `<strong>You promised Level 3–4 and then ended at the workshop.</strong> With no application projects, no coaching and no reinforcement, nothing in the architecture forces on-the-job change. You've signed up for a measurement you can't pass.`,
                    coach_r2_onewave:       `<strong>One wave of all 220 is fast but brittle.</strong> You lose the chance to pilot, learn and refine before scaling — and any design flaw hits all 220 at once. Staggered waves de-risk the launch.`,
                    coach_r2_overengineered: `<strong>A nine-module co-design can't be signed in 18 days.</strong> It's elegant, but Summit's board meets on day 18 or defaults to an off-the-shelf vendor. What's the minimum viable blueprint you can sign now and iterate after?`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>Three or four modules is too thin for this gap.</strong> You'll under-serve the coordination and decision-rights work and undercut your own impact promise. A focused 5–6 module journey is the signable sweet spot.`,
                    coach_r3_blend:         `<strong>Anchor-plus-bench is the disciplined make-vs-buy.</strong> Your 2 internal academics protect the brand-critical, dossier-integrated modules; the vetted external bench fills the rest without forcing anyone off their Q2 commitments.`,
                    coach_r3_external:      `<strong>An all-external bench is fast but expensive and shallow on the dossier.</strong> At 1.5× day-rates it erodes the margin, and external facilitators integrate weakly into the Level 3 impact dossier your brand depends on.`,
                    coach_r3_forceinternal: `<strong>Forcing both academics off their Q2 commitments breaks the schedule.</strong> You can't staff everything from two people without blowing the 90-day timeline and burning your scarcest faculty. Anchor with them; buy the rest.`,
                    coach_r3_reuse:         `<strong>Reuse-and-shift protects margin without breaking the design.</strong> Validated content assets and a cheaper live-virtual core lower cost while keeping the transfer mechanism intact — the only margin lever that doesn't damage the diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r3_leverfit:      `<strong>Faculty and margin levers are aligned.</strong> Anchoring internally and reusing validated assets on a live-virtual core holds the margin and protects the dossier-critical work at once — disciplined make-vs-buy with the diagnosis intact.`,
                    coach_r3_cutdays:       `<strong>Cutting the projects and coaching guts your Level 3 promise.</strong> You'd save cost by removing the exact mechanism that produces on-the-job behaviour change. The number improves; the impact you committed to disappears.`,
                    coach_r3_cutpax:        `<strong>Billing for 220 and training 120 is a brand-ending move.</strong> Leaving 100 managers untrained while charging the full price isn't a margin lever — it's a misrepresentation that forfeits the master agreement when it surfaces.`,
                    coach_r3_underdesign:   `<strong>Under-investing in design starves transfer.</strong> Skimp on materials, the LMS and the digital build and the application projects have nothing to run on — the Level 3 mechanism degrades. Fund the build that the promise needs.`,
                    coach_r3_overdesign:    `<strong>Over-building the platform erases the margin.</strong> A gold-plated digital build looks impressive but pushes cost up with little added transfer. Invest enough to make the projects work, no more.`,
                    coach_r3_scaled:        `<strong>Scaled-custom is the disciplined bid.</strong> Same diagnosis, leaner delivery, margin held at or above the 22% floor. You stay credible on impact while protecting the unit's P&L — the bid you can defend to both the client and your own owner.`,
                    coach_r3_full:          `<strong>Full-custom at the ceiling is a thin-margin loss-leader.</strong> It can be rational as a deliberate bet on the 3-year master agreement — but only if you priced the loss on purpose. Check that the margin didn't just disappear while you weren't looking.`,
                    coach_r3_pricetowin:    `<strong>Price-to-win on an all-external bench is margin blindness.</strong> Your contribution margin lands around 4% — well below the 22% floor. Win this and you've trained a competitor's clients at cost. Was this a deliberate loss-leader, or did the margin just vanish?`,
                    coach_r3_nobid:         `<strong>A disciplined no-bid can protect the brand.</strong> If the gap, timeline and margin genuinely can't all be met, walking away beats a programme that fails its own audit. But it forfeits the anchor relationship — be sure the trade-off is real.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That's a board-ready answer.</strong> Walking the chain from diagnosed gap to transfer design to leading behaviour indicators to the named operational KPI is exactly how you prove this moves performance, not just confidence.`,
                    coach_r4_trust:         `<strong>Reputation isn't evidence.</strong> Pointing to past satisfaction scores when the COO asks about operational performance is precisely the Level 1–2 retreat Wasatch's brand rejects. Show the chain, not the logo.`,
                    coach_r4_reframe:       `<strong>Retreating to confidence-and-engagement concedes the argument.</strong> The COO asked about operational performance; promising feelings instead admits you can't measure impact. That's the moment you lose the bake-off.`,
                    coach_r4_riskfaculty:   `<strong>Naming faculty scarcity is honest and fixable.</strong> Pre-contracting the bench and cross-training a third internal academic is a concrete mitigation a buyer can trust. Owning the real risk strengthens the pitch.`,
                    coach_r4_riskadoption:  `<strong>Adoption is the right risk to surface.</strong> Securing line-manager sponsorship and protected project time up front is exactly what makes the Level 3 transfer survive contact with the day job. Buyers trust a vendor who names this.`,
                    coach_r4_risknone:      `<strong>“No real risk” reads as naïve, not confident.</strong> A sceptical COO knows every 220-manager programme has delivery risk. Refusing to name one signals you haven't thought it through — it weakens the pitch, not strengthens it.`,
                    coach_r4_dossier:       `<strong>The renewal logic lands.</strong> A published impact dossier at 6–12 months becomes the evidence that wins preferred-provider status — you've tied this engagement to the 3-year master agreement through measured results, not discounts.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>Buying the renewal with discounts is a trap.</strong> Deep price cuts now train the client to expect them and never prove impact. Volume doesn't earn a master agreement; an auditable dossier does.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>Staying silent on renewal leaves the prize on the table.</strong> The whole point of a deliberate bid is the 3-year master agreement. Name how this engagement's evidence wins preferred-provider status.`,
                    coach_r4_lowconviction: `<strong>Low conviction invites the price-to-win comparison.</strong> If you won't firmly defend a diagnosed, measurable, feasible bid, the COO will default to the cheapest rival. Hold the line on impact discipline.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed the real gap, kept the root cause honest, committed to a defensible measure.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Designed toward the request or promised impact you can't audit.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Blended format, real transfer mechanism, staggered, signable journey.`,
